{"meta": {"course": "cat100-supplement", "chapter": "02", "section": "06", "chapter_title": "Resume, Branding, and AI Brainstorming", "title": "Revision Logs", "description": "CAT 100 textbook section connecting revision logs to revision log, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.", "learning_objectives": ["Analyze how the revision log shows a defensible decision in CAT 100.", "Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.", "Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.", "Use feedback, support requests, and revision notes to improve the artifact before sharing it."], "concept_ids": ["cat100_supplement_resume_branding_and_ai_brainstorming", "cat100_supplement_revision_logs", "evidence_based_revision", "ai_supported_learning"], "estimated_time_minutes": 14, "order": 6, "top_tier_hardened": false, "prereq_section_ids": ["cat100-supplement/02/05"], "practice_ids": ["cat100_supplement_2_6_revision_logs_artifact_signal", "cat100_supplement_2_6_revision_logs_annotation_signal", "cat100_supplement_2_6_revision_logs_ai_boundary", "cat100_supplement_2_6_revision_logs_calibration"]}, "content": "# Revision Logs\n\n![Revision Logs textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat100-supplement/02-06-revision-logs.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\n\"I revised my resume\" is a claim no one can check. A revision log turns that claim into a record: a dated, located list of what you changed, why, and what prompted each change. The skill matters in two ways. First, it makes your improvement visible to an instructor or reviewer who needs to see judgment, not just a polished final file. Second, and more practically, it protects you from your own edits; when a change turns out to be wrong, a log lets you trace back to the version that worked instead of trying to remember what you did three drafts ago.\n\nThis is the same instinct behind version control in software (Git commit messages are revision logs), behind a lab notebook in science, and behind tracked changes in a shared document. CAT 100 teaches the plain-table version so the habit is yours before the tools get fancy. Your deliverable demonstrates that you can keep a change history a stranger could read and act on.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Analyze how the revision log shows a defensible decision in CAT 100.\n- Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.\n- Use feedback, support requests, and revision notes to improve the artifact before sharing it.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nTheo changed his resume five times in a week. On Friday a mentor asks, \"the version you sent Tuesday had a line about the inventory project, where did it go?\" Theo has no idea. He overwrote the file each time, so the Tuesday wording is gone, and he cannot remember whether he cut the line on purpose or by accident. He spends an hour rebuilding something he already had. A one-line log entry (\"06/03, cut inventory bullet, felt redundant with the warehouse bullet\") would have answered the mentor in five seconds and let Theo restore the line if he changed his mind.\n\nThe case shows that the cost of not logging is paid later, in lost work and unanswerable questions. The log is cheap insurance written at the moment of the change, when the reason is still fresh.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nA usable revision log is a table with one row per change and three load-bearing columns: **what changed** (located precisely, \"Summary, line 2,\" not \"the top part\"), **why** (the reason or judgment), and **what prompted it** (the feedback, observation, or tool suggestion that triggered the edit). A date or version stamp on each row turns the table into a timeline you can walk backward.\n\nTwo properties separate a real log from a vague note:\n\n| Property | Strong log | Weak note |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Locatable | \"Experience, bullet 1: added '~80 customers/shift'\" | \"Improved my experience section\" |\n| Traceable in time | Dated rows, originals kept | One overwritten file |\n\nThe reason precise location matters is that a reviewer (or future you) must be able to find the exact spot the entry describes. The reason you keep originals rather than overwriting is that a change history only has value if the earlier states still exist; a log that points to versions you deleted is a list of ghosts. Polish does not enter here at all, the log is not meant to be pretty, it is meant to be auditable.\n\n<inline-check question=\"A log row reads \u00e2\u20ac\u02dcImproved my experience section, sounds stronger now.\u00e2\u20ac\u2122 Which load-bearing column is missing?\" explanation=\"The row gives a vague reason but never says WHAT changed or WHERE. \u00e2\u20ac\u02dcExperience section\u00e2\u20ac\u2122 is not a locatable spot, and there is no line or bullet a reviewer could open and find. Without \u00e2\u20ac\u02dcwhat changed,\u00e2\u20ac\u2122 the entry is a feeling, not a traceable record.\" options='[{\"text\":\"The locatable \u00e2\u20ac\u02dcwhat changed\u00e2\u20ac\u2122 column (no section/line named)\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"The date column only\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Nothing, it is a complete row\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"The font and formatting column\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Worked Example: Building a Three-Column Revision Log\n\nA revision log turns \"I improved my resume\" into a record a reader can trace. The reliable shape has three columns: **what changed, why, and what prompted it.**\n\n**Step 1. Capture each edit as one row, in order.**\n\n| Date | Location and change | Why / prompt |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 06/02 | Summary, line 2: \"good with computers\" to \"proficient in Excel and SQL\" | Career-center feedback flagged the phrase as vague |\n| 06/03 | Experience, bullet 1: added \"served ~80 customers per shift\" | Needed a measurable result; pulled the number from my POS reports |\n| 06/04 | Skills: removed \"Microsoft Word (expert)\" | AI tool suggested it as filler; I rejected the \"expert\" label as not defensible |\n\n**Step 2. Check that each row is locatable.** Every row names a section and a line, not \"fixed some stuff.\" A reviewer could open the resume and find each edit.\n\n**Step 3. Make the AI role visible.** Row 06/04 records that the edit started as an AI suggestion and that you rejected the inflated part. The log now shows your judgment over the tool, not just the tool's output.\n\n**Step 4. Keep the history, do not overwrite.** Because each version is dated, if the 06/03 number turns out wrong you can trace back to the 06/02 version instead of losing it.\n\nThe deliverable is the table itself: three columns, one row per change, AI decisions included.\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Log three edits the way you would in the moment\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"Career-center feedback flags your summary as vague. You change \u00e2\u20ac\u02dcgood with computers\u00e2\u20ac\u2122 to \u00e2\u20ac\u02dcproficient in Excel and SQL.\u00e2\u20ac\u2122 What is the FIRST thing the log row must pin down?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"A nicer adjective for the change\",\"The exact location: \u00e2\u20ac\u02dcSummary, line 2\u00e2\u20ac\u2122 plus the old and new wording\",\"Whether the resume font matches\",\"The reviewer\u00e2\u20ac\u2122s job title\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Right. \u00e2\u20ac\u02dcSummary, line 2: good with computers to proficient in Excel and SQL\u00e2\u20ac\u2122 is locatable. A reviewer can open the file and find the exact spot. \u00e2\u20ac\u02dcFixed the summary\u00e2\u20ac\u2122 cannot be checked.\"},{\"prompt\":\"You add \u00e2\u20ac\u02dcserved ~80 customers per shift\u00e2\u20ac\u2122 to a bullet. Beyond location, what else does the row need so it survives a question?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"A bigger, rounder number\",\"The prompt and source: needed a measurable result, number pulled from POS reports\",\"An exclamation point\",\"The reviewer\u00e2\u20ac\u2122s email\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Yes. Naming the source (POS reports) makes the number defensible. If someone asks \u00e2\u20ac\u02dcwhere did 80 come from,\u00e2\u20ac\u2122 the log already answers.\"},{\"reveal\":\"Row three is the AI move: an AI tool suggests labeling Word skills as \u00e2\u20ac\u02dcexpert.\u00e2\u20ac\u2122 You reject the inflated label and remove the line, logging BOTH the suggestion and your rejection.\"},{\"prompt\":\"Why does logging the rejected AI suggestion matter more than logging only the accepted edits?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"It makes the log longer\",\"It shows your judgment over the tool, turning \u00e2\u20ac\u02dcI used AI\u00e2\u20ac\u2122 into a defensible decision\",\"It is required by the font\",\"It hides that you used AI at all\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Exactly. The rejected-suggestion row is where your judgment becomes visible. A log of only successes hides the dead ends that prove you were steering.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\nAfter working through those three rows, put them in the order a real-time log records them: each change is captured the moment it happens, then checked for the properties that make it auditable.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"order\" items='[\"Capture the edit as one dated row the moment you make it (location + old/new wording)\",\"State the prompt and reason: what triggered it and why (feedback, a metric, an AI suggestion)\",\"Flag any AI involvement explicitly, recording what you accepted and what you rejected\",\"Preserve the prior version, never overwrite the only copy of a draft you might want back\"]'></sequence-builder>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nWrite the log entry at the moment of the change, not from memory afterward.\n\n1. **Locate the change exactly:** name the section and line, so a reader can find it.\n2. **State the reason and the prompt:** why you made it and what triggered it (feedback, a metric, an AI suggestion). One sentence each is enough.\n3. **Flag any AI involvement explicitly:** if a tool suggested the edit, record what you accepted and what you rejected, so your judgment over the tool is on the record.\n4. **Preserve the prior version:** date the rows and keep originals; never overwrite the only copy of a draft you might want back.\n\nA log you can read a month later and act on, restore a line, answer a reviewer, defend a choice, is the standard. A list of vague \"fixed things\" entries fails it.\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nOne misreading is treating the log as busywork to do at the end. A log reconstructed from memory after the fact loses the prompts and reasons that gave it value; you end up writing \"improved several lines\" because the specifics are gone. The log is a real-time tool or it is nothing.\n\nA second misreading is logging only the final, successful changes and hiding the edits you later reversed. But the reversals are often the most informative entries: they show what you tried, why it did not work, and how your judgment developed. A truthful log includes the dead ends.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nSuppose career-center feedback flags your summary as vague, and you make three edits over three days. Day one: in the summary, line two, you change \"good with computers\" to \"proficient in Excel and SQL,\" prompted by that feedback. Log it with the date, the exact location, and the prompt. Day two: in experience, bullet one, you add \"served about 80 customers per shift,\" prompted by needing a measurable result, with the number pulled from your point-of-sale reports. Log it, naming the source of the number. Day three: an AI tool suggests labeling your Word skills as \"expert\"; you reject the inflated label and instead remove the line, and you log both the suggestion and your rejection.\n\nRead the resulting table. Every row names a section and a line, so a reviewer could open the resume and find each edit. The day-three row shows the AI's role and your override, which turns \"I used AI\" into a defensible decision. Because each row is dated and the prior versions are kept, if the day-two customer count later proves wrong, you can trace back to the day-one version instead of losing it. The table itself, three columns, one row per change, AI decisions included, is the deliverable; nothing about it needs to be polished, only complete and locatable.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nA strong reader recognizes the revision log as the same idea they will meet again as Git commit messages, document version history, and lab notebooks, and treats the CAT 100 table as the training-wheels version of a career-long habit. Seeing the pattern across tools is the sign the concept landed.\n\nA strong reader also writes log entries a stranger could act on, not shorthand only they understand. \"Fixed bullet 2\" means nothing to a reviewer; \"added customer-count metric to bullet 2 from POS report\" means everything. The audience for the log is someone who was not there, and writing for that reader is the skill.\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nThe discipline of a dated, located, reasoned change history transfers anywhere edits accumulate over time. A group project benefits from a shared change log so teammates know who changed what and why; a budget spreadsheet benefits from a notes column recording each adjustment's reason; a piece of code benefits from commit messages that a future maintainer can read. The format scales from a plain table to full version-control software, but the three questions, what changed, why, and what prompted it, are the same ones you practiced on a resume here.\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat100_supplement_revision_logs\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the revision log packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat100-supplement/02-06-revision-logs-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"revision log\" course=\"CAT 100\" section=\"02.06\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[NACE career readiness](https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the revision log.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the revision log for CAT 100 02.06. A peer comment points to an interface move issue connected to [ISTE digital citizenship](https://iste.org/standards/students). Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"What would make this section more useful while you revise?\" options='[\"It asks you to name a decision, point to evidence, and choose support that helps the revision.\", \"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"0\" conceptid=\"cat100_supplement_revision_logs\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, check that the revision log records audience, evidence source, AI/software support, accepted revision, rejected suggestion, and confidence change.\n\n## References\n\n- [NACE career readiness](https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined)\n- [ISTE digital citizenship](https://iste.org/standards/students)\n- data-ink and visual evidence. https://www.edwardtufte.com/tufte/books_vdqi\n- [WCAG accessibility](https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/)\n\n", "simulation": null, "illustration": null, "practice": {"problems": [{"id": "cat100_supplement_2_6_revision_logs_artifact_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat100_supplement_revision_logs", "concept_ids": ["cat100_supplement_revision_logs", "cat100_supplement_resume_branding_and_ai_brainstorming"], "stem": "A revision log for your resume should record, for each change, three things. Which set is correct?", "options": ["The font, the color, and the file size.", "What changed, why it changed, and what evidence or feedback prompted the change.", "The date, the weather, and how long it took.", "How many words were added and how tired you felt."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "A useful revision log lets a reader reconstruct your reasoning: the change itself, the reason, and the prompt (a piece of feedback, an AI suggestion, or new evidence).", "misconception_id": "cat100_supplement_2_6_log_records_cosmetics", "difficulty": "easy"}, {"id": "cat100_supplement_2_6_revision_logs_annotation_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat100_supplement_revision_logs", "concept_ids": ["cat100_supplement_revision_logs", "evidence_based_revision"], "stem": "Which revision-log row is specific enough that a reader could find and understand the change?", "options": ["\"Fixed some stuff in the summary.\"", "\"Made it better overall.\"", "\"Summary, line 2: replaced 'good with computers' with 'proficient in Excel and SQL' after the career-center feedback flagged vague skills.\"", "\"Updated the document.\""], "correct_index": 2, "explanation": "The strong row names the location (summary, line 2), the exact change, and the prompt (career-center feedback). The vague rows could describe any edit anywhere.", "misconception_id": "cat100_supplement_2_6_vague_log_row", "difficulty": "medium"}, {"id": "cat100_supplement_2_6_revision_logs_ai_boundary", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "ai_supported_learning", "concept_ids": ["ai_supported_learning", "cat100_supplement_revision_logs"], "stem": "You used an AI tool to suggest edits. How should the revision log treat the AI's role?", "options": ["Leave AI out of the log; only the final wording matters.", "Note which edits originated from AI, which you accepted, and which you rejected, so the log shows your judgment over the tool's.", "Credit every edit to AI to be safe.", "Mention AI once at the top and never again."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "A revision log that hides AI use cannot show your judgment. Recording what AI proposed, what you accepted, and what you rejected is exactly what makes the log honest and useful.", "misconception_id": "cat100_supplement_2_6_hiding_ai_in_log", "difficulty": "hard"}, {"id": "cat100_supplement_2_6_revision_logs_calibration", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat100_supplement_revision_logs", "concept_ids": ["cat100_supplement_revision_logs", "evidence_based_revision"], "stem": "Why is a dated revision log more useful than simply saving over the old file each time?", "options": ["Dates make the document look more professional.", "It lets you and a reviewer trace the order of changes, see what feedback led to what edit, and recover an earlier choice if a later one was worse.", "It is required by every employer.", "It automatically improves the writing quality."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "The value of a log is traceability over time: you can see the sequence of decisions, link each to its prompt, and roll back if a change did not help. Overwriting destroys that history.", "misconception_id": "cat100_supplement_2_6_overwrite_loses_history", "difficulty": "medium"}]}, "content_version": {"content_version": "28e7dcab04b5cc3cc9492a1d6a7b4ccf89f6372c924fdf83ed3dcc40b0228723", "algorithm": "sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1", "parts": ["mdx", "meta", "practice", "misconceptions"]}, "misconceptions": [{"id": "cat100_supplement_2_6_log_records_cosmetics", "pattern": "log_records_cosmetics_not_reasoning", "description": "Believing a revision log should record surface details (font, color, time spent) instead of what changed and why", "trigger": "learner logs cosmetic facts but not the reason or prompt for a change", "feedback": "Log what changed, why it changed, and what prompted it. Fonts and timestamps do not show your reasoning.", "rail_action": "explain"}, {"id": "cat100_supplement_2_6_vague_log_row", "pattern": "vague_revision_row", "description": "Writing log rows so vague (\"fixed some stuff\") that a reader cannot locate or understand the change", "trigger": "learner's log entry names no location, exact edit, or prompt", "feedback": "Name the location, the exact change, and the feedback or evidence that prompted it, so a reader can follow you.", "rail_action": "practice"}, {"id": "cat100_supplement_2_6_hiding_ai_in_log", "pattern": "hiding_ai_contribution", "description": "Leaving AI-generated edits out of the log so the work looks fully self-produced", "trigger": "learner omits which edits came from a tool and which they accepted or rejected", "feedback": "Record AI's proposals and your accept or reject decisions. Hiding the tool hides your judgment, which is the point of the log.", "rail_action": "ask"}, {"id": "cat100_supplement_2_6_overwrite_loses_history", "pattern": "overwriting_destroys_history", "description": "Saving over the previous version each time instead of keeping a dated trail you can trace or roll back", "trigger": "learner keeps only the latest file and cannot reconstruct earlier decisions", "feedback": "Keep a dated trail. It lets you trace the sequence of decisions and recover an earlier choice if a later one was worse.", "rail_action": "represent"}]}